Notes and what was observed:
The first thing worth noticing is that the victim is working in finance, meaning has access to a lot of sensitive information that should not be for public or anyone that is not authorized for it.
Credentials were stolen, for the attacker to authorize deeper in the system. The credentials were used.
Attacker used a Adversary-in-the-middle attack which follows the sequences that comes next: The victim was lured by clicking on a phishing site the attacker created and the malicious proxy sits between a user and a legitimate service, in order to steal credentials as well as session tokens. 
The victim clicked then a malicious link to download a .exe file and it established a connection (C2).
What is also concerning is 4 hashes were obtained and the Mimikatz was ran to obtain said credentials via the C2 Shell. 
These 4 hashes are confirmed password hashes belonging assumingly to the victim and way it is confirming is because the attack method used was Pass-The-Hash.
It appears that also the attacker was able to export data of payment data and customer PII about over 24 thousand. Access to the SQL database.
Then a 847 mb zip file uploaded to mega.nz which means the attacker using a cloud storage service in order to host or exfiltrate data.
The C2 by the context is still active by beaconing to the IP address of the attacker, meaning there is network communication still going on. Because there is lateral movement on the system. Multiple systems are compromised, There is an active C2 still happening. 
Customer records stolen.
First of all, contact the victim, the company for further instructions on what they should do.
Change the credentials,
Protect customer records,
Disconnect and isolate confirmed compromised systems from the network using approved EDR or network controls.
Contact legal authorities.

Explanations to some attacks:

Pass-The-Hash - attacker steals a user's password hash instead of the plaintext password and reuses it to authenticate to other systems and servers.
AITM - a malicious proxy sits between a user and a legitimate service to steal session tokens or user's credentials
Mimikatz - open-source post-exploitation tool used to extract sensitive authentication credentials from Windows operating systems
Beaconing - a network communication
